WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:06.589
Let’s solve some aromaticity problems. We
have a two-part question here—we need to

00:00:06.589 --> 00:00:12.750
classify the compounds as aromatic, non-aromatic,
or antiaromatic, and draw the lone pairs on

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:19.340
heteroatoms and determine if they participate
in the π system or are orthogonal to it.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:25.130
A heteroatom is a non-carbon atom, and orthogonal
means the lone pair is perpendicular to the

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:32.369
π system; this is important because it cannot
participate in the π system when orthogonal.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:37.220
Maybe pause the video here and try to classify
these compounds on your own; when you resume,

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:39.840
we can solve the problems together.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:45.000
As a refresher, let’s review the criteria
for aromaticity. The compound must be cyclic

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:51.460
and planar. In our examples, we have relatively
small rings containing double bonds, so planarity

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:58.539
is probably not a major concern in our problems,
but it can be with larger rings. The compound

00:00:58.539 --> 00:01:05.089
must also be conjugated; the continuous π
system within the ring can be made up of sp2

00:01:05.089 --> 00:01:10.730
hybridized atoms and lone pairs. Finally,
the number of electrons in the π system must

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:18.940
satisfy Huckel’s rule, having 4n+2 π electrons.
I’ll abbreviate electrons “e minus.”

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:26.070
Now, if we replace “n” with zero or small,
positive integers, we find that our π system

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:32.920
must have a specific number of electrons to
be aromatic. We may also need to identify

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:38.300
antiaromatic compounds, which are cyclic,
planar compounds that have a continuous π

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:48.540
system containing 4n π electrons. Antiaromatic
compounds are very unstable.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.710
In problem (a), we have alternating single
and double bonds, and we can see this pattern

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:58.740
continues across two rings. We can draw a
resonance form to show electron delocalization

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:04.530
over the bicyclic compound. Remember each
of our representations is an average of the

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:09.259
actual structure, although it looks like this
bond has moved here, and this bond has moved

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:15.989
to the other ring. Let’s count π electrons.
Each double bond is made up of two electrons,

00:02:15.989 --> 00:02:26.019
so we have 2, 4, 6, 8 π electrons in this
system, which makes it antiaromatic.

00:02:26.019 --> 00:02:30.640
Moving from a hydrocarbon example to a heterocycle
with nitrogen atoms in the ring, we have a

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:36.840
bit of a different situation. Each nitrogen
has a lone pair, so we need to figure out

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:41.010
whether or not they can participate in the
π system.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:46.180
It’s helpful to look at a model for this
one. We can see the blue double bonds and

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:51.040
green single bonds, which is a reasonable
representation for the π system in this static

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:56.540
model, even though in reality delocalization
makes each bond more like a one-and-a-half

00:02:56.540 --> 00:03:03.499
bond. Notice the geometry at nitrogen. Since
it is sp2 hybridized, the lone pair must fit

00:03:03.499 --> 00:03:10.560
into this hole, which sticks out at a 120
º angle to the other bonds. The π system

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:16.959
is delocalized above and below the ring, so
the lone pairs on an sp2-hybridized nitrogen

00:03:16.959 --> 00:03:24.000
are orthogonal to the aromatic π-system.
So, this compound, called pyrazine, has three

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:29.989
double bonds in the ring, making the compound
aromatic and it also has two orthogonal lone

00:03:29.989 --> 00:03:32.299
pairs.

00:03:32.299 --> 00:03:42.780
Problem (c) has a positively charged nitrogen.
It has shared its lone pair to bond with oxygen,

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:49.209
forming an N-oxide functional group. It has
4 bonds, which gives it tetrahedral geometry

00:03:49.209 --> 00:03:56.169
and makes it sp3 hybridized. The sp3 nitrogen
atom disrupts conjugation in the ring, and

00:03:56.169 --> 00:04:02.519
this compound is non-aromatic.
Problem (d) is an interesting example. Each

00:04:02.519 --> 00:04:09.019
oxygen has two lone pairs, so each atom in
the ring has either a lone pair, or is sp2

00:04:09.019 --> 00:04:22.500
hybridized. However, this compound is non-aromatic
as drawn. Let’s examine why. We can show

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:27.940
a resonance form by pushing electrons through
the entire conjugated system, putting a negative

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:34.730
charge on the oxygen outside of the ring and
using one of the lone pairs of the ring oxygen.

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:39.070
We had to push electrons in a path that led
outside of the ring to show this resonance

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:44.690
form, thus the conjugated system is not continuous
within the ring, so the compound on the left

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:50.540
is non-aromatic. Now, the form on the right
does indeed have aromatic character, but because

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:56.310
of the charge separation, it contributes less
to the overall structure. Also, our problem

00:04:56.310 --> 00:05:02.040
asked if the compound was aromatic as drawn.
There is a second way to push electrons in

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:10.020
this system, but it also relies on conjugation
with the exocyclic double bond.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:17.190
In compound (e), we again have a sp2 nitrogen
with an orthogonal lone pair.

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:21.850
If we look at the model of this compound,
it is so strained the double bonds are bending,

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:25.620
but it certainly is planar.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:31.900
Since two double bonds make up the pi system,
this is an antiaromatic compound.

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:35.760
This compound with the positively charged
sulfur atom has three double bonds within

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:40.800
the ring. So, what about the lone pairs on
sulfur?

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:45.670
Sulfur is in the same group as oxygen on the
periodic table, so only considering the valence

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:51.420
sp hybrid orbital, we can draw a lone pair
on sulfur that does not participate in the

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:59.640
π system. This compound has 6 π electrons
and is aromatic.

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:04.080
In our final example, we should recognize
immediately that the nitrogen lone pairs are

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:10.110
orthogonal to the π system. We can draw two
lone pairs on sulfur, one of which participates

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:16.530
in the π system. Geometrically, the second
lone pair cannot participate, so it is outside

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:22.830
of the π system, though maybe not exactly
perpendicular to it. Given the two double

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:27.470
bonds in the ring and the lone pair on sulfur,
compound (g) is aromatic.

